[660.488, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step report**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Did**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I tested the assumptions with a script that loads the real page in iframes at 1280px and 390px, reads\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  layout and price output after its JavaScript has run, and re-checks after each fix. My first run was invalid\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  because the page's script hadn't executed yet, so I threw those results away and re-ran it with polling.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I looked at the phone render once after the changes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Found, and fixed**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Phone plan pick was failing.** At 700px tall, no price was visible. The fix:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The recommended Freelancer plan now comes first on phones.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The hero is tighter, with the subtitle hidden.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The billing toggle and currency selector share one row.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The phone first screen now shows the four worries, the toggle, and the Freelancer plan with its price\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and button.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The cost is that only one plan is in view at first, so the other two need a scroll.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Content was invisible with JavaScript off.** My step 1 claim that content stays visible was false. The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  fade-in effect now applies only when JavaScript is running.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Copy contradiction.** Studio listed Stripe as an integration, which implied Stripe payments were Studio-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  only. It now lists QuickBooks, Xero and Zapier, with a matching table row.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Accessibility gaps.** The table's first header cell and the currency label now have proper visually\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hidden text instead of the `hidden` attribute.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Held up**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Price math is correct in all six currency and period combinations, and there is no horizontal overflow at\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  either width.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - \"2 months free\" is exact for some combinations and slightly understated for others (up to 2.4 months), so\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I left it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Not verified**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - My check that JavaScript-off content is visible returned nothing, so that fix rests on how the CSS is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  built, not on a render.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Desktop buttons still sit below the first 720px, though the prices and plan descriptions are visible.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course:** the phone layout now puts the recommended plan first. You may prefer the original order\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and a longer scroll, so say if you want it reverted.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ▪ Step 4/8 done\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[667.356, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 5 of pushing the work further: Range. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 5/8 · Range`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[700.983, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 5/8 · Range\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What the page is really trying to do, as verbs**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Make a stranger recognize themselves in one plan.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - State the whole bill and the exit before they ask.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Remove the fear of regret, not just of cost.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Make them trust an unknown company with their money records.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Make them want it, and make the next click obvious (the genre's own verbs).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Fields, the method borrowed from each, and the direction taken** (I did this myself, without subagents)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **SaaS pricing-page designer (the genre's best practitioner).** Method: one recommended plan, self-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  identifying plan names, and \"what happens after I click\" shown next to the button. Direction taken: a short\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  strip under the plans that says what happens when you click.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Consumer-finance disclosure (the \"fee box\" and click-to-cancel rules).** Method: a uniform box with the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  same items for every product: what you pay today, when you're charged next, how to cancel, and what the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  refund rules are. Direction taken: put that box on the page in plain words, including the refund limits,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  which most pricing pages hide.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **Escrow and data-portability (the field I wouldn't normally reach for).** Method: an exit plan is a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  concrete promise, with a formats list, a time window, and no \"contact us\". Direction taken: \"After you\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  cancel you keep read-only access for 90 days and can export anytime\", instead of the vague \"export anytime\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  4. **Accessibility and plain-language editing.** Method: read the page as a screen reader and a stressed\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  non-expert would. Direction taken: replace the bare ✓ and — in the comparison table with real text for\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  assistive technology, and keep the feature column pinned when the table scrolls sideways on phones.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  5. **Red team (misuse and harm).** The results are below.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The edge I took:** \"the page that tells you the whole bill and the exit before you ask.\" Plenty of pricing\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pages are prettier. Few are this upfront about money and leaving, and for freelancers worried about lock-in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and hidden fees, that is the reason to choose it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Red team findings, including harms**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Monthly-looking price, annual charge.** Yearly is the default, so the big \"$10 / month\" is charged as\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  $120 up front, and the small grey \"Billed $120 yearly\" line is easy to miss. Fix: make that line darker and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  larger.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Unstated refund rule on yearly plans.** A 30-day guarantee plus \"cancel anytime\" invites people to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  assume unused months get refunded. Fix: state it plainly.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Trial ending.** Without a stated rule, a visitor can't tell whether a trial turns into a charge. Fix:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the new strip says nothing is charged unless you add a card and choose to continue.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Screen-reader harm.** The table's ✓ and — marks have no readable meaning for people using assistive\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tech. Fix: added text.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Financial-data trust claims.** \"Encrypted, backed up daily, never sold\" is an assertion people rely on\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with their money records. It's my placeholder. If it's wrong at launch, it is a misleading claim, not just a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  missing detail.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[711.822, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && python3 -I - <<'EOF')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[748.349, "o", "\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command, read 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Read(<workspace>/.tmpshot/o.png)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[753.962, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ The phone render shows a horizontal scrollbar. My hidden \"Included\" text in the table is probably the cause,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  so I'm containing it and re-measuring overflow.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[755.785, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && rm -rf .tmpshot && python3 -I - <<'EOF')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[784.429, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step report**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Did**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I put the five fields to work. The red team found real problems, and the page now fixes them.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **New \"What happens after you click\" strip under the plans:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - It has four steps: today is $0 with no card needed; day 14 you decide, with a reminder email and no\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  automatic charge; renewals come at the same price with a warning email; and leaving means cancel any time,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  30-day money-back, then no partial refunds.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - After cancelling you keep read-only access for 90 days and can export whenever.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - It reads as a 4-up row on desktop and stacks on phone.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Billing line:** the \"Billed $120 yearly · save $24\" line is now darker and larger on the featured plan,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  so the real charge isn't hidden behind the small \"$10 / month\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **FAQ:** the leaving answer now states the refund rule and the 90-day read-only window.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Comparison table:** the ✓ and — marks now carry \"Included\" and \"Not included\" for screen readers. The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  feature column also stays pinned when the table scrolls sideways on phones.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Found**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Placeholder policies:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The billing strip, the trial email timings, the 90-day read-only period and the encrypted/daily-backup\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  claim are all things I invented. They are flagged with `PLACEHOLDER` comments in the HTML.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Check each against your real terms before launch. If a promise on this page is false, that is a harm to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  customers, not a small copy issue.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Overflow:** a phone screenshot showed a horizontal scrollbar. The hidden accessibility text may have\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  caused it, so I contained it, and the layout check now shows no overflow at either width (375 of 390, 1265\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  of 1280).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Cut idea:** I considered a \"pays for itself\" line such as \"one late invoice paid a week sooner covers\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the plan\". I dropped it because it needs a number I can't support.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Subagents:** I didn't use any, because the five angles were quicker to run myself and the results don't\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  need independent judgement.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ▪ Step 5/8 done\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[785.766, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 6 of pushing the work further: Judge. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 6/8 · Judge`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +17 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[788.177, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 6/8 · Judge\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The checks I committed to are Lighthouse, a script for the four worries, a first-screen read at both sizes,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  a comparison against real pricing pages, and the same checks run on the first version. I'll start with the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tools I need.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
