[2582.864, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 7/8 · Stretch\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The target I set, and the stretch**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Mine, which already holds on desktop:** at least 3 of 4 worries answered, and a plan pick, in the first\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  screen.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Phone fell short of mine:** 4 titles but only 2 explanations visible, and no prices above the Freelancer\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  card.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Stretch:** on a 390×700 phone, in the first screen, all 4 worries answered with their explanation, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  all three plans identifiable by who they're for, with their prices. In other words, the whole 10-second\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  decision on the phone's first screen.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Why it's plausible:** mobile pricing pages like Basecamp's use a compact \"who are you\" selector and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  short one-line assurances. I'm borrowing those two ideas.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Where I expect it to break:** space. If the worry lines and picker rows don't fit in 700px together with\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the toggle, something has to give.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Safety:** a copy of the current page is saved outside the project, so I can revert.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
